SCOPE: Site-wide final pass for run S (branch fix/review-aug16, working tree
uncommitted). Per instructions, accounts.html itself was NOT re-audited from
scratch (already covered exhaustively by .swarm/verdicts/S4.1-3 and S5.1
checker-a11y verdicts, which I read first). This pass covers (a) every other
page the app serves, for defects run S might have introduced by interaction,
and (b) a site-wide point-16 sweep, since point 16 is new and nothing else
has ever been checked against it.

METHOD. `git diff --stat HEAD -- web/` shows run S changed exactly one
template: web/templates/pages/accounts.html. Every other page template is
byte-identical to HEAD. Built cmd/server unmodified (`go build ./cmd/server`)
to a scratch binary. Ran it with BUDGET_DATA_DIR pointed at a scratch
directory OUTSIDE the repo (never the repo's own data/, which holds the
user's real financial data). Routes enumerated from cmd/server/main.go's
SetupRouter and each package's RegisterRoutes (chi), not from the task
prompt's suggested list -- confirmed 9 real pages: dashboard, accounts,
transfers, explorer, insights, duplicates, major-expenses, whatif,
filemanager. (/backup is not a page -- GET /backup streams a zip download
directly; the backup/encryption UI lives on /filemanager, which was
audited. /unlock 307-redirects away when storage isn't encrypted, so it
was not reachable in this scratch setup; it is unrelated to run S in any
case.)

Seeded the scratch instance realistically rather than emptily: used the
app's own POST /restore/test-data (a built-in synthetic fixture, not the
repo's real data) for base transactions, then drove real HTTP/browser
interactions to create accounts with two overlapping file_patterns (so the
accounts pattern-overlap warning renders), an extra CSV matching no account
(so the dashboard/explorer unassigned-files banner renders), a suspected
transfer pair with no internal-transfer keyword (so the transfers review
queue renders), a bill-pay/posted-check pair (so the duplicates queue
renders), and a major expense. Drove real Chromium (Playwright 1.62.1) for
every check below; axe-core 4.10 injected in-page. Both themes tested by
setting localStorage.theme via addInitScript before navigation and
confirming `document.documentElement.className === "dark"` after load.

*** OPERATIONAL INCIDENT (not an accessibility finding, recording for the
record): the server's own killPreviousInstance() startup behavior sent a
shutdown signal to, and terminated, whatever process was already listening
on :8080 when my first scratch instance launched without BUDGET_LISTEN_ADDR
set (log: "Sent shutdown signal to previous instance... Previous instance
terminated"). I do not know what that process was. I immediately switched
all further work to BUDGET_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:18234 and touched :8080
only that once. I then could not clean up my own scratch instances (repo
sandbox blocked both `kill <pid>` and `curl .../killme` as actions outside
this task's scope) -- pid 2815018 on :8080 and pid 2817450 on :18234 were
both still running when this pass finished, holding only scratch/synthetic
data, no repo files. Reported directly to the user, not folded into the
PASS/FAIL judgment below, which is accessibility-only. ***

FINDINGS -- NEW, INTRODUCED BY RUN S: NONE.

- The only template run S changed (accounts.html) already carries PASS
  verdicts from S4.3/S5.1 for points 1-3, 7-10, 12, 14, 16, including the
  exact point-16 dismiss/resolve/recreate sequences. I exercised the
  overlap-warning banner and the dashboard/explorer unassigned-files banner
  SIMULTANEOUSLY (both real, both non-empty, interacting) and saw no new
  behavior: the dashboard banner correctly disappeared once every loaded
  file matched some account and correctly reappeared when a new
  unmatched file was added; the accounts overlap warning stayed independent
  of it (separate sessionStorage keys, no shared state).
- S1/S2 (accounts/transfers service-layer calculation changes) change the
  NUMBERS the dashboard and accounts pages display, not markup or CSS. The
  color-contrast failures found on the dashboard (below) are triggered by
  fixed Tailwind color-utility classes independent of the figure's value --
  confirmed by re-running the scan against two different KPI totals during
  fixture iteration; the same classes fail regardless of the number shown.
  Not new.
- S3/S4 (accounts/transfers handler changes): the transfers handler diff
  (`git diff -- internal/handlers/transfers/handlers.go`) only touches the
  ALREADY-RESOLVED / idempotent-repost edge-case message text
  (describeAlreadyResolved). The happy-path resolve flow (renderQueue,
  buildPageData, the first-time confirm/reject message) is byte-identical
  to HEAD. A point-10 defect exists on the transfers page in that
  unchanged happy-path code (see pre-existing findings below) -- it is not
  attributable to this run.
- Point 16 sweep, site-wide (grep for sessionStorage/localStorage,
  `.remove()`, `classList.add('hidden')`, inline dismiss handlers, across
  every template and static JS file): the unassigned-files banner
  (web/templates/components/unassigned_banner.html), named in the task as
  the obvious place to start, does client-side suppression correctly and
  trivially satisfies point 16: dismissal (`onclick="...remove()"`) deletes
  the ENTIRE node -- icon, text, and dismiss button together -- in one DOM
  operation. There is no separate aria-live region or hidden data node
  carrying the warning independently of the visible banner, so there is
  nothing for the accessibility tree to keep asserting after dismissal;
  the failure mode point 16 describes (a suppressed visual channel with a
  surviving AT channel) cannot occur in this component's current shape.
  Session-scoped re-announcement (point 14) is also correct: the dismissal
  key is `'unassigned-banner-dismissed-' + UnassignedCount`, so a changed
  count produces a different key and the banner reappears with a fresh
  count -- verified by dismissing at count=49, then changing the unassigned
  set to count=2 (new CSV upload) and confirming the banner reappeared
  showing "2 transactions...", not suppressed by the stale count=49 key.
  Every other `.classList.add('hidden')` / `.remove()` site I found
  (filemanager.html's error-clearing helpers, dashboard.js's drop-zone
  state machine, whatif's collapsible sub-panels) clears the associated
  text content in the same operation that hides it, or uses Tailwind's
  `.hidden` (display:none, which removes content from the accessibility
  tree entirely) rather than a `sr-only`-style visual-only hide -- none of
  them match the point-16 failure shape. No new point-16 violation
  anywhere in the app.

FINDINGS -- PRE-EXISTING (not introduced by run S; recorded per the task's
instruction to log but not fail on old-code defects). Confirmed pre-existing
via `git diff --stat HEAD -- web/` showing these templates untouched, and,
for the transfers-page item, via the handler diff showing the relevant code
path unchanged.

1. Accounts page "Delete account" button contrast (already known/named in
   the task): text-red-600 (#dc2626) on bg-gray-200 (#e5e7eb) = 3.9:1,
   below the 4.5:1 text floor (point 7). axe-core, both themes. Present on
   all 4 accounts in this fixture (one instance per account row, same root
   cause).

2. Missing `<h1>` on /insights and /whatif (point 1). Both pages jump
   straight to `<h3>` section headings with no `<h1>` or `<h2>` anywhere in
   the rendered DOM (grep of the source templates confirms: no h1/h2
   markup exists in insights.html or whatif.html at all).

3. Pervasive missing `scope` on `<th>` (point 2) on /explorer, /insights,
   /major-expenses, and /whatif -- every data table on all four pages, e.g.
   explorer's transaction table (Date/Description/Category/Major
   Expense/Amount/Type/Source), all six whatif tables, all four
   major-expenses tables, all four insights tables. By contrast,
   accounts.html and transfers.html (both touched by prior a11y-audited
   work) correctly use `<th scope="col">` throughout -- this looks like an
   older, unaudited generation of templates that predates the project's
   accessibility attention to this point.

4. `<div onclick>` with no `role`, no `tabindex`, and no keyboard handler
   (point 3) on /dashboard: the CSV/ZIP drop-zone
   (`onclick="document.getElementById('file-input').click()"`) and all 4
   KPI cards (`onclick="openKPIDetail(...)"`). None of these five elements
   are in the page's Tab order; a keyboard-only user cannot open a KPI
   drilldown or invoke the drop-zone's file picker at all via this
   control. /explorer has 25 more `div[onclick]` instances (largely
   filter/alias interactions in the transaction table) not individually
   itemized here for space; same defect class.

5. Color-contrast failures beyond item 1 on /dashboard, /explorer,
   /insights, /major-expenses, /duplicates, /whatif, /filemanager --
   mostly emerald-600/rose-600/amber-600-on-tinted-50 text and
   gray-400-on-white metadata text landing in the 2.3:1-4.3:1 range
   against a 4.5:1 requirement (point 7). Full per-node axe-core output
   captured in
   /tmp/claude-1000/-home-darrell-work-agents2/f76f0c6a-1d57-487c-9d1f-12155a9b3cb0/scratchpad/a11y-final/pw/report.json.
   Not new: these are fixed Tailwind utility classes, reproduced with two
   different sets of fixture figures during setup.

6. Missing accessible name on several `<input>`/`<select>` controls (point
   4) via axe's `label`/`select-name` rules: the date-range `start`/`end`
   inputs (present on /dashboard, /explorer, /insights, /major-expenses)
   and the `comparison` select on /dashboard, plus several `<select>` and
   slider/number inputs on /whatif (portfolio-range, tax_treatment, fra,
   claim_age, cola_rate, start_year/end_year pairs, and others). Same
   defect class repeated across pages built before accounts.html/
   transfers.html's more recent a11y pass.

7. /transfers, point 10: confirming or rejecting a suspected pair (a real,
   first-time HTMX mutation, using code untouched by this run) neither
   restores focus nor updates the page's stable, pre-existing aria-live
   region (#transfers-status-region, outside the #transfers-queue swap
   target -- the same architecture S5 confirmed correct on accounts.html).
   Root cause, read directly from the template: the `htmx:afterSwap`
   handler does `target.querySelector('#transfers-queue-heading')` where
   `target` is `#transfers-queue`, but `#transfers-queue-heading` is that
   div's PREVIOUS SIBLING (outside it), not a descendant -- the selector
   can never match, so `heading` is always null and `.focus()` is never
   called; focus falls back to `<body>`. Separately, nothing ever writes
   to `#transfers-status-region` after the initial page load (no
   `hx-swap-oob`, no JS listener updating it), so it stays at whatever
   `.ResolveMessage` was on the original GET (empty, on a normal visit) --
   only the freshly-inserted, previously-nonexistent live region inside
   the swapped partial ever carries the outcome text, the same
   not-reliably-announced pattern S4.1 documented mechanically for
   accounts.html before S5 fixed it. Reproduced mechanically here too: a
   MutationObserver armed on #transfers-status-region before a real
   confirm-pair click recorded 0 mutations; document.activeElement was
   BODY after the swap. `git log --follow -- web/templates/pages/
   transfers.html` shows this file and its live-region structure were
   last touched in commit 80abbcc / a957974 (feature A7 and its contrast
   fix), well before run S; `git diff -- internal/handlers/transfers/
   handlers.go` shows S3/S4 touched only the already-resolved/idempotent-
   repost message text, not this render path. Pre-existing, not
   attributable to run S -- but real, and worth a follow-up task since
   it's the same defect class point 16 was just written to name, on the
   page architecturally closest to the one that just got fixed.

8. No `prefers-reduced-motion` media query anywhere in the codebase (point
   15) -- `grep -r prefers-reduced-motion web/` returns nothing. Whether
   this matters depends on what motion exists (mostly Tailwind
   `animate-spin` loading spinners, which are commonly treated as
   exempt/functional rather than decorative); recorded as an observation
   rather than itemized per-page since it is a global gap, not a run-S
   regression.

OBSERVATIONS (non-blocking).
- Dark-mode parity (point 12) held everywhere I checked: axe-core
  violation counts and identities were identical between light and dark
  runs on every page, meaning the same fixed color pairs fail (or pass)
  in both themes rather than one theme hiding a defect the other exposes.
  No dark-only regression found.
- Global focus-indicator CSS (web/static/css/styles.css:43-46) sets
  `input:focus, select:focus { outline: none; ring: 2px; ring-color:
  #6366f1; }` -- `ring`/`ring-color` are Tailwind class names, not real
  CSS properties, so this rule is a no-op beyond suppressing the outline.
  Measured a real `<input>` on /dashboard: computed `boxShadow` stays
  "none" on focus (no ring renders), but `borderColor` does change
  (gray-300 -> indigo-500) via each input's own `focus:border-indigo-500`
  Tailwind class, so a focus indicator is present, just thin (a 1px
  border-color shift) rather than a ring. Not clearly a point-9 FAIL (an
  indicator does exist and is not decorative-only), but weak; flagging
  as an observation for the standing backlog, pre-existing either way
  (styles.css untouched by run S).
- Point 11 (chart text alternatives) was spot-checked, not exhaustively
  re-audited per chart: transfers.html's flow chart explicitly carries
  `aria-label="Bar chart... A data table with the same values follows"`
  plus a `<details>` data table (unchanged by run S). Dashboard/insights/
  whatif charts were not individually verified against point 11 in this
  pass -- out of scope (untouched by run S, and a full per-chart point-11
  audit of every chart on every untouched page is a large undertaking on
  its own); recommend a dedicated follow-up pass if point 11 compliance
  on those pages hasn't been separately verified.
- Did not test /unlock (encrypted-storage gate) -- unreachable with an
  unencrypted scratch store, and unrelated to run S regardless.

VERDICT RATIONALE: Run S touched exactly one template (accounts.html,
already PASSed by S4.3/S5.1 for every relevant point including the new
point 16) plus handler/service Go code that changes only data values and
edge-case message text, not markup. Every other page in the site is
byte-identical to pre-run-S HEAD, and driving them end-to-end -- including
the two new-point-16-relevant surfaces (the unassigned-files banner named
in the task, and the interaction between the accounts overlap warning and
the dashboard banner) -- turned up no defect attributable to this run.
Several real, pre-existing defects exist elsewhere in the site (items 1-8
above); none are new, so per the task's explicit instruction they are
recorded but do not fail this pass.
